The constituency name refers to the [[Devon]] towns of [[Honiton]] and [[Sidmouth]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Reporter |first=Ollie Heptinstall-Local Democracy |date=2023-07-06 |title=New 'Honiton and Sidmouth' constituency among final Boundary Commission proposals |url=https://seaton.nub.news/news/local-news/new-honiton-and-sidmouth-constituency-among-final-boundary-commission-proposals-191075 |access-date=2024-05-18 |website=Seaton Nub News |language=en}}</ref> It is considered by [[BBC News]] to be a battleground between the Conservatives and Liberal Democrats.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-05-24 |title=What voters want in Devon election battleground |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c6pp92845ylo |access-date=2024-06-09 |website=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref>

== Boundaries ==
{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Honiton and Sidmouth (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries from 2024}}
The constituency will be composed of the following wards (as they existed on 1 December 2020):

* The District of East Devon wards of: Axminster; Beer & Branscombe; Coly Valley; Dunkeswell & Otterhead; Feniton; Honiton St. Michael’s; Honiton St. Paul’s; Newbridges; Newton Poppleford & Harpford; Ottery St. Mary; Seaton; Sidmouth Rural; Sidmouth Sidford; Sidmouth Town; Tale Vale; Trinity; West Hill & Aylesbeare; Yarty.
* The District of Mid Devon wards of: Cullompton North; Cullompton Outer; Cullompton South.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule 1 Part 7 South West region}}</ref>

It will comprise the following areas:<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=New Seat Details - Honiton and Sidmouth |url=https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/calcwork23.py?seat=Honiton%20and%20Sidmouth |access-date=2023-07-29 |website=www.electoralcalculus.co.uk}}</ref>

* The towns of [[Axminster]], [[Honiton]] and [[Seaton, Devon|Seaton]] and the surrounding rural areas of [[East Devon|East Devon District]], transferred from the constituency of [[Tiverton and Honiton (UK Parliament constituency)|Tiverton and Honiton]] (to be abolished)
* The towns of [[Ottery St Mary]] and [[Sidmouth]] in East Devon District transferred from the constituency of [[East Devon (UK Parliament constituency)|East Devon]] (to be abolished)
* The town of [[Cullompton]] in [[Mid Devon|Mid Devon District]], also transferred from Tiverton and Honiton
Following a local government boundary review in Mid Devon which came into effect in May 2023,<ref>{{Cite web |last=LGBCE |title=Mid Devon {{!}} LGBCE |url=https://www.lgbce.org.uk/all-reviews/mid-devon |access-date=2024-04-04 |website=www.lgbce.org.uk |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=The Mid Devon (Electoral Changes) Order 2021 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2021/889/contents/made}}</ref> the constituency will now comprise the following from the 2024 general election:

*The District of East Devon wards of: Axminster; Beer & Branscombe; Coly Valley; Dunkeswell & Otterhead; Feniton; Honiton St. Michael’s; Honiton St. Paul’s; Newbridges; Newton Poppleford & Harpford; Ottery St. Mary; Seaton; Sidmouth Rural; Sidmouth Sidford; Sidmouth Town; Tale Vale; Trinity; West Hill & Aylesbeare; Yarty.
* The District of Mid Devon wards of: Cullompton Padbrook; Cullompton St Andrews; Cullompton Vale; Lower Culm (part); and very small parts of Bradninch and Halberton wards.<ref name=":0" />

