{{Short description|Parliamentary constituency in the United Kingdom, 1997 onwards}} {{Use dmy dates|date=April 2022}} {{Infobox UK constituency main |name = Forest of Dean |parliament = uk |image = |caption = |image2 = |caption2 = |map1 = ForestOfDean2007 |map2 = EnglandGloucestershire |map_entity = Gloucestershire |map_year = 2010 |map_size = 150px |year = 1997 |abolished = |type = County |previous = [[West Gloucestershire (UK Parliament constituency)|West Gloucestershire]] |next = |elects_howmany = One |year2 = 1885 |abolished2 = 1950 |next2 = [[West Gloucestershire (UK Parliament constituency)|West Gloucestershire]] |type2 = |elects_howmany2 = One |electorate = 71,510 (2023)<ref>{{cite web |url= https://boundarycommissionforengland.independent.gov.uk/2023-review/the-2023-review-of-parliamentary-constituency-boundaries-in-england-volume-two-constituency-names-designations-and-composition/the-2023-review-of-parliamentary-constituency-boundaries-in-england-volume-two-constituency-names-designations-and-composition-south-west/#lg_forest-of-dean-cc-71510 |title= The 2023 Review of Parliamentary Constituency Boundaries in England – Volume two: Constituency names, designations and composition – South West |publisher=Boundary Commission for England |access-date=27 June 2024 |df=dmy }}</ref> |mp = [[labour|Matt Bishop]] |party = [[labour|Labour]] |region = England |county = [[Gloucestershire]] |towns = |national = |westminster = |european = South West England }} '''Forest of Dean''' is a [[Constituencies of the Parliament of the United Kingdom|constituency]]{{#tag:ref|A [[county constituency]] (for the purposes of election expenses and type of returning officer)|group= n}} in [[Gloucestershire]] represented in the [[British House of Commons|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]] since 2024 UK General Election by Matt Bishop, a [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] MP. Previously represented from [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005]] by [[Mark Harper]], a [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] who has served as [[Secretary of State for Transport]] since 2022.{{#tag:ref|As with all constituencies, the constituency elects one [[Member of Parliament]] (MP) by the [[first past the post]] system of election at least every five years.|group= n}}
== Boundaries == {{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Forest of Dean (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame=yes|frame-width=220|frame-height=220|text=Map of current boundaries}} '''1885–1918''': The Sessional Divisions of Coleford, Lydney, Newent, and Newnham.
'''1918–1950''': The Urban Districts of Awre, Coleford, Newnham, and Westbury-on-Severn, the Rural Districts of East Dean and United Parishes, Lydney, Newent, and West Dean, and part of the Rural District of Gloucester.
'''1997–2010''': The District of Forest of Dean, and the Borough of Tewkesbury wards of Haw Bridge and Highnam.
'''2010–2024''': The District of Forest of Dean, and the Borough of Tewkesbury ward of Highnam with Haw Bridge. The constituency boundaries remained unchanged.
'''2024-present''': Further to the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], enacted by the Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023, the composition of the constituency from the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]] will be unchanged.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule 1 Part 7 South West region}}</ref>
== History == This seat was created for the [[1885 United Kingdom general election|1885 general election]] (replacing the two-seat constituency of [[West Gloucestershire (UK Parliament constituency)|West Gloucestershire]] under the [[Redistribution of Seats Act 1885]]), was redrawn for the 1918 general election, and abolished for the [[1950 United Kingdom general election|1950 general election]]. It was re-created, with different boundaries, for the [[1997 United Kingdom general election|1997 general election]], and has thus far not undergone any boundary changes.
== Constituency profile == The Forest of Dean constituency covers Gloucestershire west of the river Severn, and lies in the south west of England, near the Welsh border.
The core of the constituency consists of the Royal [[Forest of Dean]] itself, which was established by [[William the Conqueror]] nearly a thousand years ago and is one of the last surviving Royal Forests in England.<ref>{{cite web |title=Forest of Dean |url=https://www.gloucestershirelive.co.uk/all-about/forest-of-dean |publisher=Gloucestershire Live |accessdate=21 August 2020 |archive-date=1 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200801070201/https://www.gloucestershirelive.co.uk/all-about/forest-of-dean |url-status=live }}</ref> The seat has a rich industrial and mining history, evidenced by the market towns of [[Coleford, Gloucestershire|Coleford]] and [[Cinderford]], and the old port of [[Lydney]] from where coal mined in the [[Forest of Dean Coalfield]] would start its journey to all parts of the world.<ref>A Glance Back at Lydney Docks, Neil Parkhouse {{ISBN|9781903599006}}</ref>
The Wye Valley forms the western border of the Forest and is an area of outstanding natural beauty, whilst the [[River Leadon|Leadon Valley]] forms the northern portion of the constituency. The Vale consists of countryside and farmland centred on the Tudor town of [[Newent]], and also produces English wine.
The constituency also includes parishes from [[Borough of Tewkesbury|Tewkesbury]] district, including Forthampton, Chaceley Hole, Hasfield, Ashleworth and Highnam.
